  "debriefMetadata": {
    "summary": "A 54-year-old smoker with 90 minutes of typical ischemic chest pain in a PCI-capable ED. The case trains rapid ECG acquisition, guideline initial therapy, and time-critical cath-lab activation — and shows how delay, not just wrong choices, harms the patient.",
    "keyTeachingPoints": [
      "Obtain and read the 12-lead ECG within 10 minutes of arrival for acute chest pain (EV-STEMI-010)",
      "Reperfusion decisions are clinical + ECG decisions — do not wait for troponin (EV-STEMI-011)",
      "At a PCI-capable center, primary PCI is the reperfusion strategy: activate the cath lab immediately once the ECG is diagnostic (EV-STEMI-020, EV-STEMI-021)",
      "Give the aspirin loading dose early; load a preferred P2Y12 inhibitor without delaying reperfusion (EV-STEMI-030, EV-STEMI-031)",
      "Avoid what does not help: routine oxygen at normal SpO2, NSAIDs in ACS, and fibrinolysis when the cath lab is available (EV-STEMI-033, EV-STEMI-038, EV-STEMI-023)"
    ],
    "commonErrors": [
      "Completing a broad workup (labs, X-ray, long history) before the ECG",
      "Waiting for the troponin result before activating the cath lab",
      "Treating pain with an NSAID",
      "Giving a fibrinolytic despite an available cath lab",
      "Choosing a disposition before activating reperfusion"
    ]
  },
